{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs","id":1161577,"title":"Mages & Flat Experience Costs","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-22T12:04:45-08:00","id":1161577,"title":"Mages & Flat Experience Costs","content":"I wasn't really a fan of flattening the experience curve for Chronicles of Darkness for supposed simplification reasons. I think it's pretty silly given the complexity of the rest of the game with Conditions, Beats, in depth combat rules, 3 types of damage, complex social combat rules and with Mage in particular, the spell system.<br /> <br /> It has the odd effect of making everyone want to super specialize. Why would you buy Time 1 when you can buy Death 4?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T12:22:26-08:00","id":1161583,"title":"","content":"Exponential XP costs like what we used to have aren\u2019t actually complicated. They\u2019re just something that makes it take longer to get to the upper limits of a gameline. If you consider that XP is actually split between mundane and arcane then there\u2019s the complexity and it was kept from one edition to another.<br /> <br /> Also, what\u2019s wrong with being a specialist?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T13:39:08-08:00","id":1161600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs#post1161577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wasn't really a fan of flattening the experience curve for Chronicles of Darkness for supposed simplification reasons. I think it's pretty silly given the complexity of the rest of the game with Conditions, Beats, in depth combat rules, 3 types of damage, complex social combat rules and with Mage in particular, the spell system.<br /> <br /> It has the odd effect of making everyone want to super specialize. Why would you buy Time 1 when you can buy Death 4?</div> </div> </div> </div>In my experience the hyper specialization is actually not that much of a problem because of the Gnosis requirements. Yes, players do rush to Gnosis 3 because in a lot of ways that is where the game really begins and then may even get an Arcanum at 4. Then however the cost of raising Gnosis further to unlock potential Mastery seems to be a very steep climb to them so they rather broaden their Arcana arsenal at that point and tend to sit at Gnosis 3 for a long time. This is further amplified by Legacies and the cost associated with getting those running.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I also had someone who started with a generalist spread and this also payed off by the sheer amount of extra Mysteries he can scrutinize and therefore earn Arcane Beats from. In a long term campaign (and assuming no Group Beats) the generalist is likely going to pull ahead on total Experience earned.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T14:46:19-08:00","id":1161615,"title":"","content":"With the flat costs you could actually play a PC you want to portrait. It makes it possible to be good on something and you don't need to be super-specialized from char gen if you want to be a specialists. The reason to buy Time 1 instead of Death 4 is that it fits the character. If you have a problem with powergamers, thats a table problem.<br /> <br /> It still is possible to be a generalist but the game doesn't fuck you over if you actually want to be good at something anymore.<br /> <br /> For all chronicles we played in V:tR 1ed. Most of them played for longer than a year. No one ever bought anything above 3 dots, and not a single player over the years payed a single exp into buying blood potency. It was just to expensive compared to generalize. No one bought anything expect for maybe one or two dots in disciplines and all the rest exp was into merits and skill dots at low dots during those chronicles. <br /> Since 2ed , we've got many more interesting, broad and specialized concepts that could actually be backed up with dots and exp as well.<br /> In mage though, as sailbot already pointed out about the Arcana vs Gnosis, makes it a non-problem.<br /> <br /> <br /> The greatest problem in my mind with char-gen is that you could increase powerstat by sacrificing merits. That's just sad in my book, that's so much 'hero-without-a-past&quot; as it goes. (no, no one has ever considered it around my table, so it's not a problem for me, just a nuisance). <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/523-poseur","name":"Poseur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"375"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T15:02:08-08:00","id":1161619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs#post1161577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wasn't really a fan of flattening the experience curve for Chronicles of Darkness for supposed simplification reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Who supposed that?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs#post1161577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it's pretty silly given the complexity of the rest of the game with Conditions, Beats, in depth combat rules, 3 types of damage, complex social combat rules and with Mage in particular, the spell system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Almost like &quot;simplification&quot; wasn't the reason xp costs were flattened.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs#post1161577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It has the odd effect of making everyone want to super specialize. Why would you buy Time 1 when you can buy Death 4?</div> </div> </div> </div>My experience has been exactly the opposite. Exponential xp costs made everyone want to super specialize because generalizing with your free character creation dots was objectively a waste of xp, and because if you wanted Death 4 you'd have to save up all of your xp for like a year of play time, so you'd better not go wasting it on low-level stuff. With flat costs, there's less benefit to min/maxing, and generalizing isn't a trap option for chumps any more.<br /> <br /> That said, if you want to use exponential xp costs in your game, it's easy to do. Multiply the 2e experiences per dot cost by 5 to get the cost of the third dot in 1e experience points. Divide that by three to get the cost of the 1st dot in 1e experience points. Multiply that by 2 to get the cost of the second dot, four to get the cost of the fourth dot, and five to get the cost of the fifth dot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 11-22-2017, 06:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T16:18:41-08:00","id":1161643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161619#post1161619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My experience has been exactly the opposite. Exponential xp costs made everyone want to super specialize because generalizing with your free character creation dots was objectively a waste of xp, and because if you wanted Death 4 you'd have to save up all of your xp for like a year of play time, so you'd better not go wasting it on low-level stuff. With flat costs, there's less benefit to min/maxing, and generalizing isn't a trap option for chumps any more.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't have a lot to add here, but having played several hundred hours of 2E Mage (as a player/ST both) I just want to echo Charlaquin's sentiment here, as it also reflects my experience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T17:45:08-08:00","id":1161669,"title":"","content":"Well I'm glad I started this thread. You guys have made some solid arguments in favor of the changes in 2E. Admittedly, I have only played about 30 hours of 2E and I only have 1 player with Gnosis 2 even. I suppose the Gnosis 3 &quot;sweet spot&quot; could encourage diversification and it's certainly possible in a long game to reach high levels of Gnosis now, so you guys have convinced me that it was probably a good change. Remember to mark the day that someone changed someone's mind on an Internet forum. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Math time.<br /> <br /> My players are averaging 6 beats per 3 hour session. About 4 regular and 2 arcane. I could see some acceleration in arcane after you get a 2nd Obsession and hit exceptional successes with your praxes more often.<br /> <br /> My typical chronicle runs 40 sessions or so. I could see getting 160 regular and 100 arcane beats over the chronicle.<br /> <br /> Gnosis 5 = 4 x 25 = 100 beats<br /> Primary Arcana 5 = 2 x 20 = 40 beats<br /> Secondary Arcana 4 = 2 x 20 = 40 beats<br /> Third Arcana 3 = 2 x 20 = 40 beats<br /> That would leave 20 beats for Legacy attainments and maybe 20 beats = 4 XP for other merits and skills.<br /> <br /> So my players might be Gnosis 5, Master, Adept, Disciples with Legacies and more status in their Orders.<br /> <br /> That's definitely more powerful than my 1e 48 session game where my players ended up as Gnosis 4, Adept, Disciple, Apprentice.<br /> <br /> PS: I like my players to be powerful as I feel power is a big theme of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1470,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","user":"Johnny Awesome","date":". 11-22-2017, 08:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T18:08:50-08:00","id":1161671,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs#post1161577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It has the odd effect of making everyone want to super specialize. Why would you buy Time 1 when you can buy Death 4?</div> </div> </div> </div>In-game time would be the greatest factor.<br /> Getting the first dot in a sphere might take just a month, getting the fourth one should require like 4 years. 1 month of downtime can be easy to get in a chronicle, but 4 years not so much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T19:43:56-08:00","id":1161717,"title":"","content":"While my very limited playtime with 2e (~20hours) will probably cloud better judgement, I find that mage still has specialization issues with the Arcana. That even if you are a Moros with your Death and Matter, that you still will probably at the very least want Prime 2 for Counterspell/magic armour. Maybe Space 2 if you are casting sympathetically. Perhaps even Fate/Time for hung or conditional spells. There is just too many nice things spread out. The cheaper costs may tempt you to be scarier in having that 4th dot and access to more deadly things, but depending on what you are facing, being more spread out in your Arcana is probably going to be a safer tactic.<br /> <br /> In 1e, the exponential costs felt like in itself imposed learning times (by the time it took you the 24xp for Death 4, and perhaps another 32xp for gnosis 4, enough time could have passed for you to learn that stuff) where as 2e, it seems to be more on the ST to say how long magic takes to learn. I could see how the flat rate should say 'I have the xp for Death 4, I should be able to spend it' instead of Death 4 maybe taking years of IG learning.<br /> <br /> As an aside, and perhaps it is just me, but I did also find that the whole beats and conditions systems a bit distracting in that things seem to encourage you to go out of the way to get things that would result in a beat. From things that might not make total sense like taking an dramatic failure to resolving conditions themselves - why should being good at something in a moment give you a beat. Maybe it is just me in that it feels too gamey in ways that takes from the story bits when gaining xp is part of the rules rather than something the ST hands out at the rate they feel people should be gaining at.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12014-peraxis","name":"Peraxis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T20:03:38-08:00","id":1161725,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161671#post1161671\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In-game time would be the greatest factor.<br /> Getting the first dot in a sphere might take just a month, getting the fourth one should require like 4 years. 1 month of downtime can be easy to get in a chronicle, but 4 years not so much.</div> </div> </div> </div>What are you basing that on?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T02:04:53-08:00","id":1161792,"title":"","content":"WoD manuals always stated (and when not, implied) that as the number of dots raised it also raised the difficulty to learn and the required time. If Computer 1 is &quot;touchscreen&quot;, easy as shit and can be learnt in a few days, Computer 3 is &quot;programmer&quot; and you need either a couple years of studies to understand how to code in Java and make websites; to reach 5 you need several more years of practice, learning and high-end researches.<br /> <br /> The time esteem is mostly based on Ascension rules (where the first dot in a Sphere takes 1 month to learn while the fifth one requires 10 years, RAW), I can't say if they ever wrote such a rule for Awakening too (I didn't read all the supplements) but back then they wrote that &quot;learning magic is supposed to be hard and take time, people should not turn into a Master overnight&quot;; I think it makes sense to apply the same philosophy to Awakening too.<br /> <br /> I don't remember every exact numbers anyway, I think it was like 1 month/ 3 months/ 1 year/ 5 years/ 10 years.<br /> <br /> That would mean that a mage with Prime 2, Fate 3 and Forces 5 has studied magic for about 20 years and he's about 40 years old. Prime 2, Fate 5 and Forces 5 would bring it around 35 years of studies and 55 years old - that for a master of two different spheres sounds quite right. A young mage could reach Prime 2, Fate 2 and Forces 2 in about 1 year overall.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T02:07:26-08:00","id":1161794,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161792#post1161792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">WoD manuals always stated (and when not, implied) that as the number of dots raised it also raised the difficulty to learn and the required time. If Computer 1 is &quot;touchscreen&quot;, easy as shit and can be learnt in a few days, Computer 3 is &quot;programmer&quot; and you need either a couple years of studies to understand how to code in Java and make websites; to reach 5 you need several more years of practice, learning and high-end researches.</div> </div> </div> </div>You realize that the dots aren't raised.... that's the whole point of the thread. So based on this line of thinking, each dot of arcana would take the same amount of time.<br /> <br /> But regardless... This isn't Old World of Darkness....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 11-23-2017, 05:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T02:28:43-08:00","id":1161797,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161794#post1161794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You realize that the dots aren't raised.... that's the whole point of the thread. So based on this line of thinking, each dot of arcana would take the same amount of time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't follow, probably because your phrase lacks precision.<br /> The point is that the <i>experience cost</i> is not raised, no one is talking about the time. I wrote about computers because it's just obvious how there's a difference in the required time between learning 1 and learning 3. And yes, I am very aware this is CoD thank you, I'm simply assuming that the required time increase is somewhat similar because it makes more sense than the alternative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T02:40:40-08:00","id":1161801,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161797#post1161797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't follow, probably because your phrase lacks precision.<br /> The point is that the <i>experience cost</i> is not raised, no one is talking about the time. I wrote about computers because it's just obvious how there's a difference in the required time between learning 1 and learning 3.</div> </div> </div> </div>It takes as long to take the dots as it takes to get the dots. There is no minimum amount of time required, especially not when it comes to &quot;learning how to master a thing that doesn't exist in real life&quot; like getting a dot in an arcanum. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And yes, I am very aware this is CoD thank you, I'm simply assuming that the required time increase is somewhat similar because it makes more sense than the alternative. </div> </div> </div>It's just, using stuff from a completely different game system isn't a very strong argument for assuming a mechanic exists.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T03:21:11-08:00","id":1161810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161801#post1161801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It takes as long to take the dots as it takes to get the dots. There is no minimum amount of time required, especially not when it comes to &quot;learning how to master a thing that doesn't exist in real life&quot; like getting a dot in an arcanum.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yes it takes as long as it takes. However one can presume it takes longer to buy higher dots then lower ones no matter what trait it is. What time are we talking about though is the real question.<br /> I think DaveB have stated sometime that it shouldnt take more then a few years to reach first degree master if you're into it. If we're talking Arcana. Though it doesn't exist in real life doesn't mean it is of no importance in game. One should consider what would be normal if you study it, timewise, before being allowed to buy the dot, no matter what trait. <br /> Like everyone probably agrees that going to the gym 1 week and then dumping 10 exp to raise Athletics to 5 is a bad idea because it's unreasonable for a slacking coach potato to reach olympic levels of athleticism in one week. Same reasoning should apply toward things like Arcana and vampire Disciplines etc. even though these things are complete fiction.<br /> <br /> Me for instance would probably use a rule of thumb that with more or less full time study, including first hand experience with the inner workings of the arcana, it would take a couple of months to get a forth dot from the third. No matter what happened, I would not allow my players to raise it to forth dots the day after the bought their third, no matter if the have the &quot;Experience&quot; or not.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/523-poseur","name":"Poseur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"375"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T04:05:23-08:00","id":1161817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161801#post1161801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It takes as long to take the dots as it takes to get the dots. There is no minimum amount of time required, especially not when it comes to &quot;learning how to master a thing that doesn't exist in real life&quot; like getting a dot in an arcanum.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is just your opinion and I believe it makes no sense, no offense meant. Your take is something more in line on how videogames work rather than role playing games and it's bound to lead to weird and shonen-like situations where the 15-years old boy is just as powerful as the ancient masters. &quot;It's magic so it doesn't need to make sense&quot; is also a quite trite syllogism that has only brought lousy narrative up to now. =P<br /> <br /> You get 1 beat every scene where a mentor teaches you. If you had a lessons for each in-game day, like Hogwarts, you'd reach your master's level at the end of the first three months. Three scenes with three different masters on three different subjects, like it would properly work in an organized school, and you'll have six arcana at 5 by the end of the year. Am I the only one to see how it would makes no sense at all?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Like everyone probably agrees that going to the gym 1 week and then dumping 10 exp to raise Athletics to 5 is a bad idea because it's unreasonable for a slacking coach potato to reach olympic levels of athleticism in one week. Same reasoning should apply toward things like Arcana and vampire Disciplines etc. even though these things are complete fiction. </div> </div> </div>Exactly. You can't just skip common sense because you have an high enough number (=exp).<br /> As far as I can see it, the issue with the Awakening flat progression is more an issue of how the players are using it rather than the system itself: if you refuse to take time and circumstances into account then your characters will level up in a silly way. That's your fault though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13673,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","user":"Maris Streck","date":". 11-23-2017, 07:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T04:38:08-08:00","id":1161824,"title":"","content":"There is a very big difference between &quot;Getting an extra dot is going to take whole years to accomplish&quot; and &quot;you get it all done in one day&quot;.<br /> <br /> Getting the beats and experience to not only get all the dots of an arcana would take some time, especially because it will also require gnosis dots. But there is no reason why that would require the game needing to take place over a ridiculously large span of time rather than the decent amount of time it took to get all of those experiences.<br /> <br /> I'm not advocating &quot;ignore logic&quot;, I'm advocating &quot;It takes time to get beats and experience and often you'll be spending that on things like rotes and merits and skills along the way so by the time you get it via the games progression system, it'll probably make sense that you got it&quot;. That makes so much more sense to me than &quot;You don't get to play a mage basically because our campaign takes place on a day by day scale rather than each chapter being many months apart&quot;.<br /> <br /> Also, where is &quot;You get 1 beat every scene where a mentor teaches you&quot; written? I don't recall seeing it in the merit section.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 11-23-2017, 07:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T05:07:44-08:00","id":1161827,"title":"","content":"Overinflated mandatory training times just bog down games. Yeah, it's &quot;realistic&quot; but seriously, fuck that shit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T05:09:15-08:00","id":1161828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161827#post1161827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Overinflated mandatory training times just bog down games. Yeah, it's &quot;realistic&quot; but seriously, fuck that shit.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Constructive. But whatever floats your boat ^_^","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/523-poseur","name":"Poseur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"375"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T05:17:42-08:00","id":1161832,"title":"","content":"Requiring more XP to increase skills makes sense from a realistic perspective, but for a game system it's not great. The way the core system worked, a 5th dot was a lot less useful than that first dot, so making it five times as expensive was an issue.<br /> <br /> Mage simply follows that central design. I can see why a designer wouldn't want two XP systems.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T07:55:59-08:00","id":1161869,"title":"","content":"The whole idea of how Mages grow is by following their Obsessions and throwing themselves into Mysteries to gain first hand experience. Mages can even artificially increase their rate of Beat acquisition by inflicting themselves with Conditions. The Mage willing to experience and risk more will outstrip his peers, and it doesn\u2019t really map to artificial training time for dots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T08:36:24-08:00","id":1161877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161817#post1161817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is just your opinion and I believe it makes no sense, no offense meant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, no, it's the Awakening RAW. Awakening has no learning times either mechanical or vague-narrative ones.<br /> <br /> It also makes perfect sense in the context of how magic works in Awakening. Magic is not simply a skill you improve with practice and practice, and more practice. As a very Gnostic themed approach, Awakening handles magical advancement in terms of experiential knowledge of the Supernal via Mysteries rather than traditional concepts of skill improvement. You improve your Arcana and Gnosis by lived experiences (generating Beats and Experiences) that expand your intuitive conceptualization of the Supernal Truth. There is no logic or reason to how fast someone can accomplish this, because it isn't a matter of logic or reason.<br /> <br /> Mages that go out and investigate the Mysteries of the world develop much faster than a mage that sits around studying ancient tomes, because they open themselves to a much wider array of mysteries and insights leading to gnosis (instead of regular knowledge). Reading books about magic is a narrow set of experiences and thus only allows a narrow trickle of insights.<br /> <br /> Also, it's a game and a setup that makes going out and doing stuff better than sitting around studying in a library is kind of a 'duh' thing to include.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your take is something more in line on how videogames work rather than role playing games and it's bound to lead to weird and shonen-like situations where the 15-years old boy is just as powerful as the ancient masters. </div> </div> </div>Mysteriously potent youths are a very common trope in the mythologies and media Mage is based on; enough that any overlap with another genre is irrelevant.<br /> <br /> Awakening is not a game about playing a novice magician who might overpower any mundane individual through magic, but is dwarfed by the ancient masters in who's games you are but a pawn.<br /> <br /> Awakening is a game about the line between a novice and true master is not the magical feats they can achieve, but the wisdom they have acquired in how to wield the power they possess. It is the Sorcerer's Apprentice writ larger, where the apprentice can enchant his cleaning equipment to life to do his chores for him, but lacks the understanding of consequences to realize why he shouldn't have done something beyond so beyond his current skills in controlling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You get 1 beat every scene where a mentor teaches you. If you had a lessons for each in-game day, like Hogwarts, you'd reach your master's level at the end of the first three months. Three scenes with three different masters on three different subjects, like it would properly work in an organized school, and you'll have six arcana at 5 by the end of the year. Am I the only one to see how it would makes no sense at all? </div> </div> </div>You're not honestly applying the rules, which is why they don't make sense in application.<br /> <br /> First off, this rule only applies to a Legacy tutor and their initiates. So you have to join a Legacy (costs 1 Arcane XP and Gnosis 2 in the first place...) before this comes into play. Hogwarts isn't a Legacy.<br /> <br /> Second, Scene-logic is not meant to be applied to downtime-logic. 99% of class time in Hogwarts in the Harry Potter books is not in any scene, because that class time doesn't matter. So none of that class time would count. Only Scenes count, which means only narratively significant events count. Three months of Divination classes with zero events worthy of a Scene means zero progress towards another dot in Time or Fate.<br /> <br /> Third, gaining an Arcane Beat in this fashion doesn't just require a Scene of instruction it requires, &quot;...they interact over some mystically meaningful or emotional and intimate matter...&quot; Since learning the Arcana and developing Gnosis is a matter of direct experience with Mysteries, the tutor's role is to help guide their students to Mysteries (and specifically the ones the Legacy was built to engage with), and shape those explorations into a meaningful experience. Harry showing off his learning of a new spell to Snape's disapproval of his arrogance of naivety might make for a Scene, but despite Snape being a teacher and Harry a student, it doesn't mean an Arcane Beat would be generated.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Exactly. You can't just skip common sense because you have an high enough number (=exp). </div> </div> </div>However, there's a big difference here. In the CofD 2e advancement paradigm, having a high number of Experiences is directly related towards not having to worry about &quot;common sense,&quot; esp. regarding Arcane Experiences and raising Arcana/Gnosis. The player-facing methods of generating Beats means you end up with high numbers by doing the stuff that would pass the common sense smell test for you learning a new trait. The ST only needs to step in when players try to buy things that don't make sense with the events of the game, otherwise the learning experiences happened when you gained all the Experiences.<br /> <br /> ---------<br /> <br /> Also, you're wrong about the RAW for Ascension anyway. While Ascension has provided <b>optional</b> estimates of how long it takes to learn things, they are clearly not supposed to apply to player characters, and NPCs break them all the time. The actual RAW in the WoD regarding how fast it takes to learn something is that you can't raise something by more than one dot at a time; and generally not more than once per story. Even in Ascension character advancement follows the will of the plot, not some appeal to &quot;realism.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161832#post1161832\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Requiring more XP to increase skills makes sense from a realistic perspective,...</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it doesn't.<br /> <br /> There is no XP system that makes sense from a realistic perspective on learning. Humans don't learn in a fashion that's conducive to a mechanical advancement system in a game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T09:23:30-08:00","id":1161893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161877#post1161877\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, it doesn't.<br /> <br /> There is no XP system that makes sense from a realistic perspective on learning. Humans don't learn in a fashion that's conducive to a mechanical advancement system in a game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for your irrelevant input.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T09:54:39-08:00","id":1161903,"title":"","content":"It might not be relevant to the rest of what you said (I don't disagree with the rest of what you said), but it is very relevant to the discussion on XP and other advancement systems.<br /> <br /> Games are meant to be fun. For a significant number of RPG players, a serious effort towards verisimilitude is a key part of having fun with the game. Appeals to &quot;realism&quot; - esp. ones that aren't actually a serious consideration of how reality works to our current best understanding thereof - hamper discussions and designs by causing friction between fun mechanics that are &quot;realistic&quot; enough to satisfy the drive for verisimilitude, and unfun mechanics that are overly foucsed on the conflation between realism and verisimilitude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T11:09:59-08:00","id":1161922,"title":"","content":"Yes. I don't impose artificial time limits on advancement, but just require some reasonable explanation as to an increase and during the course of a story it's almost always easy to justify raising something by a dot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T11:49:09-08:00","id":1161930,"title":"","content":"The linear-chargen/quadratic-advancement combo in the oWoD and nWoD 1e systems imply that the only practical way to get a trait above 3 dots is by buying it in character creation - and therefore the mechanically optimal PC starts as an absurdly narrow specialist, only to broaden in play. Using linear methods in both character creation and advancement avoids favoring specialists over generalists; a starting PC can be a generalist without being doomed to watch the specialists zoom past him to greater power. That's the real reason why CofD advancement is linear.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1133-michael-brazier","name":"Michael Brazier","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T12:18:37-08:00","id":1161937,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161903#post1161903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It might not be relevant to the rest of what you said (I don't disagree with the rest of what you said), but it is very relevant to the discussion on XP and other advancement systems.<br /> <br /> Games are meant to be fun. For a significant number of RPG players, a serious effort towards verisimilitude is a key part of having fun with the game. Appeals to &quot;realism&quot; - esp. ones that aren't actually a serious consideration of how reality works to our current best understanding thereof - hamper discussions and designs by causing friction between fun mechanics that are &quot;realistic&quot; enough to satisfy the drive for verisimilitude, and unfun mechanics that are overly foucsed on the conflation between realism and verisimilitude.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, but you didn't put the effort in to explain that and you still haven't. If you want to say that it's actually nowhere near realistic, have at it, but don't just nitpick what is clearly a rhetorical device; that's not going to convince anyone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T12:43:38-08:00","id":1161941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161877#post1161877\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, no, it's the Awakening RAW. Awakening has no learning times either mechanical or vague-narrative ones.<br /> <br /> It also makes perfect sense in the context of how magic works in Awakening. Magic is not simply a skill you improve with practice and practice, and more practice. As a very Gnostic themed approach, Awakening handles magical advancement in terms of experiential knowledge of the Supernal via Mysteries rather than traditional concepts of skill improvement. You improve your Arcana and Gnosis by lived experiences (generating Beats and Experiences) that expand your intuitive conceptualization of the Supernal Truth. There is no logic or reason to how fast someone can accomplish this, because it isn't a matter of logic or reason.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it does mix well with the Awakening feeling. That said, you are supposed to make roughly 1 xp each session. Since you can level up Arcana through general beats you'll probably do so every 4 session, on an average. If you don't get downtime during them you could step up to master in one week of IC time. I can get how from-zero-to-hero may be very appealing to some, but it just seems absurd to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First off, this rule only applies to a Legacy tutor and their initiates. So you have to join a Legacy (costs 1 Arcane XP and Gnosis 2 in the first place...) before this comes into play. Hogwarts isn't a Legacy. </div> </div> </div>Hogwarts <i>could </i>be a Legacy. And Gryffindor is one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Second, Scene-logic is not meant to be applied to downtime-logic. 99% of class time in Hogwarts in the Harry Potter books is not in any scene, because that class time doesn't matter. So none of that class time would count. Only Scenes count, which means only narratively significant events count. Three months of Divination classes with zero events worthy of a Scene means zero progress towards another dot in Time or Fate. </div> </div> </div>That is obvious, but the difference between downtime and playtime was not what the exemple was supposed to point out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Third, gaining an Arcane Beat in this fashion doesn't just require a Scene of instruction it requires [...] </div> </div> </div>Everything related to magic can be &quot;mystically meaningful&quot;. The line you quoted only adds that you can add an Arcane beats even to magic unrelated topic if they happen to relate to handling the character's emotional sphere (this is getting closer and closer to Hogwarts, isn't it).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, you're wrong about the RAW for Ascension anyway. While Ascension has provided <b>optional</b> estimates of how long it takes to learn things, they are clearly not supposed to apply to player characters, and NPCs break them all the time. The actual RAW in the WoD regarding how fast it takes to learn something is that you can't raise something by more than one dot at a time; and generally not more than once per story. Even in Ascension character advancement follows the will of the plot, not some appeal to &quot;realism.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Yeah, no. Firstly: every rule is optional.<br /> <br /> Second: M20 core manual writes that out that learning Spheres &quot;takes significant amounts of downtime \u2013 the time characters spend offstage in between adventures \u2013dedicated to learning and practicing new principles and techniques.&quot; It also adds that you can't increase any trait by more than a dot each time, that you should not raise it if you didn't do enough to raise it during sessions and it further suggests to raise Spheres and other traits with long improvement times only during downtimes.<br /> <br /> Since it didn't give a more precise time frame, though (or if it did I don't own the manual), I'm borrowing the one descripted in &quot;The Bitter Road&quot;, pag 73: &quot;Storytellers should remember that developing the Spheres takes a <i>long </i>time. As a rule of thumb, you can use the times below or you can establish your own baselines: 1 month/ 3 months/ 1 year / 5 years / 10 years.<br /> These times may seem rather lengthy, and they are. One doesn't master magic overnight&quot;<br /> <br /> It <u>clearly</u> is supposed to apply to player characters, but thanks for your input.<br /> <br /> Third, and I really need to stress this out, no amount of rules can replace common sense. You want to be both the rule lawyer and the casual fun player, I get it and I don't really care since you're not playing with me. You also need to realise, thought, that since White Wolf games are both simulationist and adopting a modern setting it's inevitable to include a certain degree of realism. You can play however you want, don't complain if the games appear silly though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T14:36:40-08:00","id":1161971,"title":"","content":"So, M20 has nothing to do with MtA.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T16:21:10-08:00","id":1161999,"title":"","content":"What it boils down to is that this is a question of ST fiat. There are no clear rules on the subject in Awakening afaik, so learning Arcana takes as long as the ST thinks makes sense. Given Awakenings metaphysics I can certainly see gnostic revelations unlocking an Arcanum dot. Something like that should be wrought into play by the ST and it might be a bit &quot;special&quot;... but PCs are special people afterall. Generally I agree with the sentiment that higher dot levels should take more time to learn though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T18:52:08-08:00","id":1162038,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161893#post1161893\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanks for your irrelevant input.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">\u200bThat doesn't seem like it's respecting other users or the conversation. Let's not make this a habit please.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Onyx Path Forum Administrator</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">Posts in this color are moderator posts</span></b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\"><span style=\"color:#00FF00\">Posts in this color mean a Great Old One has driven me mad.</span></span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11-darksider","name":"Darksider","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11_2.jpeg","title":"Forum Administrator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1692"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T23:09:42-08:00","id":1162102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161971#post1161971\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, M20 has nothing to do with MtA.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ofc not, I only borrowed the time span under the assumption that higher levels require more time, may they be Arcana or Attributes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T04:10:30-08:00","id":1162138,"title":"","content":"I think most people here just don't think quick advancement is going to be a problem. Considering that you need to buy 2 Gnosis and the 5th dot to get Mastery in an Arcana. It's 14 game sessions essentially. 28 from the beginning of the chronicle if you have a single-minded focus.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T07:23:58-08:00","id":1162168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162102#post1162102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ofc not, I only borrowed the time span under the assumption that higher levels require more time, may they be Arcana or Attributes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since they\u2019re not related to each other it shouldn\u2019t be assumed that the material in one accurately describes how things should work in the other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T09:17:15-08:00","id":1162198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162168#post1162168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since they\u2019re not related to each other it shouldn\u2019t be assumed that the material in one accurately describes how things should work in the other.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not, just borrowed the values. There's enough material in Awakening to leave the CWOD alone and still support my theory.<br /> <br /> The difference between the Science 4 and 5 in real life is roughly 5 to 10 years of high-end professional practice in the best research labs in the world, so we can safely assume that you shouldn't be able to buy your fifth Science point when the in-game time advanced by two weeks, even if you already have the experience to do so. The cost for increasing a skill was also flattened in Awakening 2nd edition and there's no rule stating that it should be harder or longer to learn Science 5 rather than Science 1, yet common sense tells us so.<br /> <br /> So, it should also not be assumed that the flattening of experience cost of Arcana means that level 5 is just as difficult or long to learn than level 1. It's a comparison that is proved false for every other trait in the game, so what we're left is a baseline showing how having enough exp is not a sufficient condition to allow the trait increase: it is just a necessary one. Also, Awakening 1st edition had an incremental cost for Arcana just like for everything else, so it makes sense to assume that in this setting even magic was designed to be harder and harder to learn, at least in theory.<br /> In 2nd edition the exp system has changed, but the philosophy of the setting has not.<br /> <br /> Also, don't forget that Arcana can be bought with general experience while Gnosis only with Arcane beats, so you could theorically raise Arcana without doing a single thing related to magic and that has really little to do with how gnostic magic should work; it implies that Arcana are raised through mundane studies and not just revelations.<br /> <br /> In the end I have no raw and my take on the issue may still be wrong; assuming that &quot;you take as long as to get the exp&quot;, though, is clearly wrong. So, unless you can offer me some point of view that can hold <u>at least</u> enough &quot;maybe&quot; to stand on equal footing with my ideas I say there's no way I'm changing my mind and I've nothing else to discuss.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162138#post1162138\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think most people here just don't think quick advancement is going to be a problem. Considering that you need to buy 2 Gnosis and the 5th dot to get Mastery in an Arcana. It's 14 game sessions essentially. 28 from the beginning of the chronicle if you have a single-minded focus.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that's fine, if fast progression works better for you then by all means use it. If you still wonder why should someone get Time 1 rather than Death 4, though, the answer may be just lie in the fact that you're considering the exp as sufficient condition instead than as a necessary one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13673,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","user":"Maris Streck","date":". 11-24-2017, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T10:51:17-08:00","id":1162217,"title":"","content":"Just a point of clarification: Gnosis can be bought with either type of experience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T12:32:18-08:00","id":1162237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161941#post1161941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">thought, that since White Wolf games are both simulationist and adopting a modern setting it's inevitable to include a certain degree of realism. You can play however you want, don't complain if the games appear silly though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah no, White wolf is not simulationist, its pride itself on being &quot;narrative&quot; and story focused. And any game that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162198#post1162198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">it should be harder or longer to learn Science 5 rather than Science 1, yet common sense tells us so.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here is the problem, common sense has very little to do with WoD in many regards. The premise of the game is that we play normal people that &quot;awakened&quot; &quot;changed&quot; &quot;were embraced&quot; etc. Into the world of darkness. Yet i can make the Strongest man of the world who happens to also be the best scientist in the world.<br /> <br /> Even the correlation between what the numbers means is kind of wonky once you take into account bonuses.<br /> <br /> Your arguments and that of <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1470_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1470\" data-vbnamecard=\"1470\">Johnny Awesome</a> remind me of my first Werewolf the apocalypse DM. It was a big pbp game and the DM was very unfront saying &quot;i dont want stupid things like 18 year old elders&quot; so no matter if we technically had the renown to pass rank we couldnt until he felt like it.<br /> <br /> Now, I think you are approaching this from the wrong angle. Maybe intentionally or not the current XP is more simpler for the DM. I can judge how much XP i wanna give to player in an entire campaign with less variables to consider. So if think &quot;I am gonna give them a grant total of 250 XP in the whole campaign&quot; I can calculate that is X amount of attribute point or merit, or skill, etc. <br /> <br /> With scaling cost that calculation becomes way more complex because then i gotta factor in the fact that raising attributes have different costs. And all that work worth it? For a meaningless difference between having Skill/Attribute 3 or 5? Which is just more dices to the pile. <br /> <br /> Also it generates a gameplay problem in which the PC have to save that XP for long period of time just to buy the 5th dot in something which isnt really fun. This player feels trapped on his XP and just want to finally reach and spending for the unsastisfying 5 dot in something<br /> <br /> Now another player started running mage and took the basic idea of &quot;no 18 year old masters&quot; and said &quot;okay guys, this game is gonna be broke down into arc and you cannot raise Spheres beyond the cap of the arc. So arc 1, we were cap at sphere 2. Then there was a 5 years time skip and the new cap was Sphere 3 and so on. By the end we were 40 year old veterans (the game started with out PC in high school) with Sphere 4.<br /> <br /> While both put a cap in the name of story preferences, the latter option is more fun to play with. Why? Because knowing that after the arc i am gonna be able to spend the XP (which is little in the case of awakening, in ascension the DM gave us free xp in the timeskip) to advance in the arcanum meant my XP wasnt taken hostage by the XP piggy bank and i could spend it in other things like other arcanums at low levels. Or even purely flavorful skills like Finances or merits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T17:50:10-08:00","id":1162294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Poseur</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161810#post1161810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yes it takes as long as it takes. However one can presume it takes longer to buy higher dots then lower ones no matter what trait it is. What time are we talking about though is the real question.<br /> I think DaveB have stated sometime that it shouldnt take more then a few years to reach first degree master if you're into it. If we're talking Arcana. Though it doesn't exist in real life doesn't mean it is of no importance in game. One should consider what would be normal if you study it, timewise, before being allowed to buy the dot, no matter what trait.<br /> Like everyone probably agrees that going to the gym 1 week and then dumping 10 exp to raise Athletics to 5 is a bad idea because it's unreasonable for a slacking coach potato to reach olympic levels of athleticism in one week. Same reasoning should apply toward things like Arcana and vampire Disciplines etc. even though these things are complete fiction.<br /> <br /> Me for instance would probably use a rule of thumb that with more or less full time study, including first hand experience with the inner workings of the arcana, it would take a couple of months to get a forth dot from the third. No matter what happened, I would not allow my players to raise it to forth dots the day after the bought their third, no matter if the have the &quot;Experience&quot; or not.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> At the same time, you could plug in the exp and be given a time frame for when that skill should be available for use.<br /> <br /> Also, natural story progression can allow for time skips. Not to mention the use of magic to try and commit skills to memory much faster (if the ST is inclined on permitting fast-tracking, but couching it within a magic modified extended action over a period of time). It probably works best with mind and life, but I imagine you can get creative with other arcana.<br /> <br /> It makes perfect narrative sense to me for Thyrsus or Mastigos to learn physical and mental skills faster than normal people. Heck, Thyrsus and Mastigos can even employ the aid of spirits and goetia to help them learn.<br /> <br /> I know what you're getting at, though, because I have trouble believing a previously unskilled fighter could just decide they want to learn Aikido and just dump exp into it to become a master overnight. That's why I would either give them a time frame, or ask them if they'd like to talk about naturally acquiring it little by little during time-skips, or if they'd like to employ supernatural methods for learning it at supernatural speed.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, you'd be trying to learn a human skill, as a human, but at a supernatural rate. You could at least supply some supernatural reasons for why you can be an Olympic skater before someone who has been practicing diligently for 10 years.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think I would just ask what makes the most sense, then try to find a compromise for the player - because it is just a game and we want to get to fun stuff.<br /> <br /> D&amp;D naturally does with with leveling up, where you suddenly acquire a huge stack of new spells or abilities and feats. I always thought it was jarring, but I also think people are willing to suspend their disbelief pretty easily.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T19:37:58-08:00","id":1162323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162217#post1162217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a point of clarification: Gnosis can be bought with either type of experience.</div> </div> </div> </div>huh. You're right, I remembered wrong. My bad.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162237#post1162237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah no, White wolf is not simulationist, its pride itself on being &quot;narrative&quot; and story focused. And any game that.</div> </div> </div> </div>They certainly put a lot of stress on the intepretative part but they do not look narrative to me. Too many rules, too many rolls and tables, narrative RPGs are something like Nobilis. Simulationism does not mean that the story is not relevant, just that the stories are created by following the natural consequences of the actions rather than choosing the effects that makes the better story. Of course you can play any game as narrative or simulative since it's not strictly dependant by the game system, but the rules are more oriented on simulation. Some games more than others, though: Masquerade is basically demanding simulation while 3rd edition Exalted looks one of their most narrative games (disclaimer: didn't play it yet so my impressions my be wrong).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your arguments and that of <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1470_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1470\" data-vbnamecard=\"1470\">Johnny Awesome</a> remind me of my first Werewolf the apocalypse DM. It was a big pbp game and the DM was very unfront saying &quot;i dont want stupid things like 18 year old elders&quot; so no matter if we technically had the renown to pass rank we couldnt until he felt like it. </div> </div> </div>I can understand the feeling. Most age situations in Apocalypse can be solved through the use of downtimes, hard trials to level up (becoming elder requires a lot of attempts and a difficult task, after all) and designing the scenes so the players don't always gain renown but also have good chances to lose it. Or the master of challenge could simply sneer at you saying &quot;go back sucking your mom's tit, kid&quot;, after all he's the one deciding who goes up.<br /> <br /> In my first Apocalypse campaign the ST didn't allow us to gain permanent renown more than once per game (moots are once a months, after all) and we could only spend exp during downtimes; the first one was 3 IC years, so we started the second part of our chronicle with a different family situation, rank 2 and a lot of points more in our character sheet. And it made perfectly sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T19:53:39-08:00","id":1162328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162323#post1162323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They certainly put a lot of stress on the intepretative part but they do not look narrative to me. Too many rules, too many rolls and tables, narrative RPGs are something like Nobilis. Simulationism does not mean that the story is not relevant, just that the stories are created by following the natural consequences of the actions rather than choosing the effects that makes the better story. Of course you can play any game as narrative or simulative since it's not strictly dependant by the game system, but the rules are more oriented on simulation. Some games more than others, though: Masquerade is basically demanding simulation while 3rd edition Exalted looks one of their most narrative games (disclaimer: didn't play it yet so my impressions my be wrong).</div> </div> </div> </div>I should note, I said &quot;pride itself&quot; not &quot;pull it off&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162323#post1162323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my first Apocalypse campaign the ST didn't allow us to gain permanent renown more than once per game (moots are once a months, after all) and we could only spend exp during downtimes; the first one was 3 IC years, so we started the second part of our chronicle with a different family situation, rank 2 and a lot of points more in our character sheet. And it made perfect sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think is a good way to handle the 18-year-old Masters, in a way that allows for a certain setting preference (that is of old people get to be master of an arcanum) and is conducive to play. Something that the previous XP system (scaling cost) wasn't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T20:51:57-08:00","id":1162336,"title":"","content":"18-year-old Masters are only a problem if you feel they are. There is no shortage of fiction about incredibly powerful youth. Learning quickly can be indicative of being talented, a genius, prophecy/destiny, external influence or having latent potential.<br /> <br /> You can decide for yourself if you're okay with a story focused on quick-learners, the talented, and geniuses - or want to table things at a more slow and realistic pace.<br /> <br /> This kind of goes along with the idea of your troupe being among the few cabals that regularly makes tons of progress and advances its goals steadily while many of the NPCs stagnate in the background as a point of reference. I don't strive to keep upgrading NPC stats and getting new equipment and spells for them unless it is related to the story. Even still, NPCs won't progress as much as PCs do. PCs naturally have the &quot;spark&quot; and motivation to figure things out. Whereas NPCs are typically replaced with more competent NPCs (more resourceful or dangerous enemies and allies) or upgraded (merits/resources), if possible.<br /> <br /> Also, being talented or a genius can attract all sorts of admirers and haters - at the end of the day, quick-learner or not, you can still get your legs broke.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T21:06:51-08:00","id":1162343,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162328#post1162328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I should note, I said &quot;pride itself&quot; not &quot;pull it off&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yea, I get it but it's not really true. I mean, take a look at Dogs in the Vineyard and compare it to the VtM manual, the difference in is striking; and I'm not talking about the sheer volume of paper but at the philosophy behind the dice rolls. If you wanted to make a comparison, it's the difference between the concept of rolling for attack and rolling for Withering damage (it's an Exalted 3 rule, should you not know what I'm talking about).<br /> <br /> Awakening is more narrative than Masquerade and Masquerade has a greater focus on the story than every mainstream game that came earlier (D&amp;D, MERP, Rolemaster, Cyberpunk...) so it's not like they can't take pride in that; but as far as I can see is not meant to be a narrative game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-25T04:33:10-08:00","id":1162431,"title":"","content":"OK, back home after the holiday and ready to retread a bit:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1161941#post1161941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since you can level up Arcana through general beats you'll probably do so every 4 session, on an average.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a fairly poor assumption. Spending all your XP on one Arcana is great and all, but it's hardly the be all and end all of the game. You've given up on a ton of other things to spend XP on to do it, and are not an inherently superior character for having driving straight into specializing in one Arcana as fast as possible. And you only can do this with two Arcana out of ten no matter how much XP you have until you start needing tutors just to justifying being able to spend.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you don't get downtime during them you could step up to master in one week of IC time. </div> </div> </div>I don't think I've ever played in a non-Convention game (aka no expectation of long term play anyway) where you'd cover seven in-game days over eight gaming sessions. That's not just an issue with XP, that's an issue with all sorts of aspects of the baseline CofD 2e paradigm and time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can get how from-zero-to-hero may be very appealing to some, but it just seems absurd to me. </div> </div> </div>Nobody - including the game - is forcing a particular pacing on you. If you want slower going, slow things down... really that simple. The system is designed to be flexible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hogwarts <i>could </i>be a Legacy. And Gryffindor is one. </div> </div> </div>Hogwarts most certainly could not. It's a boarding school, not a mystic philosophy that requires you to alter your Gnosis itself to even join. Hogwarts Houses aren't any more Legacies, because they're just kids being sorted by a hat; not them actively reshaping their whole approach to magic.<br /> <br /> Or another way: Hogwards and the Houses within do not specialize in anything resembling a specific Arcanum that would translate as the Legacy's Ruling Arcanum. Likewise, they teach nothing that would imply Legacy specific Attainments (though Hogwarts teaches plenty of rotes), Yantras (not entirely fair as the Potterverse treats the equivalents of Yantras as inherently universal so you can only teach people how to be good at the existing ones, not make up new ones), or Oblations.<br /> <br /> If you want to point to something similar to a Legacy from the Potterverse, you need to use something like the Death Eaters (unique spells/custom variations on them, close sympathetic bonds to the Legacy founder/primary initiator, etc.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Everything related to magic can be &quot;mystically meaningful&quot;. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Can be&quot; doesn't mean it will be every time.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Firstly: every rule is optional. </div> </div> </div>No. Every rule is subject to the golden rule/rule zero.<br /> <br /> This is decidedly different from a rule the books themselves list as optional (as in not one accounted for as the default playstate of the game).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since it didn't give a more precise time frame, though (or if it did I don't own the manual), I'm borrowing the one descripted in &quot;The Bitter Road&quot;, pag 73: &quot;Storytellers should remember that developing the Spheres takes a <i>long </i>time. As a rule of thumb, you can use the times below or you can establish your own baselines: 1 month/ 3 months/ 1 year / 5 years / 10 years.<br /> These times may seem rather lengthy, and they are. One doesn't master magic overnight&quot; </div> </div> </div>Lordy that's misrepresenting things.<br /> <br /> First off that &quot;rule of thumb&quot; or &quot;establish your own,&quot; is pretty important as something not to be cited as &quot;default RAW.&quot;<br /> <br /> Second, you're leaving out the whole rest of that side-bar that talks about the level of time invested in actual practice those numbers entail (aka some, but not super consuming), and how you can then lower those times via Backgrounds. An Ascension mage with access to a decent Library and Mentor can master a Sphere in about the same time a mage trying to figure it all out on their own can learn the second dot (1+ successes on the Library check, 3+ sucesses on the Mentor check is a minimum reduction from 10 years to 7.5 months).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You also need to realise, thought, that since White Wolf games are both simulationist and adopting a modern setting it's inevitable to include a certain degree of realism. </div> </div> </div>No, I don't. I've been at this for over 20 years now.<br /> <br /> WW and now Onyx Path (which is what matters for this thread anyway) have always rejected the GNS/Big Theory formulation in their design (keeping in mind that the GNS model was developed explicitly to complain about how VtM was a &quot;Storytelling Game&quot; but didn't focus enough in the rules on storytelling).<br /> <br /> The games aren't designed on that modality of thinking. They're not &quot;narrative&quot; or &quot;simulationist,&quot; they're their own style of creative agenda. As well, they are going to include a degree of attention towards verisimilitude, not realism. The games don't care about modelling guns accurately, but modelling them in a fashion that makes them feeling close enough to guns for the setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can play however you want, don't complain if the games appear silly though. </div> </div> </div>I'm not complaining about the games &quot;appearing&quot; silly.<br /> <br /> I'm objecting to the assessment that if you use the rules in the most silly possible interpretation, that the games are at fault for silly results compared to using the rules with their given intention.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162198#post1162198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The difference between the Science 4 and 5 in real life is roughly 5 to 10 years of high-end professional practice in the best research labs in the world, so we can safely assume that you shouldn't be able to buy your fifth Science point when the in-game time advanced by two weeks, even if you already have the experience to do so.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're apparently not done borrowing from the WoD then.<br /> <br /> The CofD do not assign real world benchmarks to the Skills to say how long it should take to get from 4 to 5. This isn't the WoD where you need four dots to be a doctorate in a Knowledge. Five dots is simply human level mastery with no linked association to something to say it should take the kind of effort you've described.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The cost for increasing a skill was also flattened in Awakening 2nd edition and there's no rule stating that it should be harder or longer to learn Science 5 rather than Science 1, yet common sense tells us so. </div> </div> </div>Increasing form zero to 1 is five times slower/harder than increasing from zero to 5.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In 2nd edition the exp system has changed, but the philosophy of the setting has not. </div> </div> </div>In any matter of which this is true, the 2e system generally realizing the philosophy of the setting-as-game better; even if not to your specific tastes.<br /> <br /> Though there have big some pretty significant changes to the philosophy of the setting anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...assuming that &quot;you take as long as to get the exp&quot;, though, is clearly wrong. </div> </div> </div>How so? Lots of games do this. I'd wager to say most games do this (even if not XP or not solely XP): when you have the resources to increase traits, you get to increase traits. At most they take on something like, &quot;at a break in the action,&quot; or something.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you still wonder why should someone get Time 1 rather than Death 4, though, the answer may be just lie in the fact that you're considering the exp as sufficient condition instead than as a necessary one. </div> </div> </div>Or, you know, the player whats what Time 1 offers, since Death 4 doesn't give you the same things.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162323#post1162323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Too many rules, too many rolls and tables, narrative RPGs are something like Nobilis.</div> </div> </div> </div>The &quot;weight&quot; of the rules has nothing to do with the GNS theory designation of the kind of playstyle intended. You have rules heavy narrative games with lots of rules, rolls, and charts, because it's the focus of what the rules do that matters.<br /> <br /> It's also kind of strange that you're falling back on this when the specifics of the thread (XP) in the CofD are heavily focused on active player choice and the Beat economy... which is dominated by what most people consider a more narrative design style (willingly accepting negative situations/consequences that might not be optimal or even 'realistic' in the name of the genre and story of the games is the primary motivator of Beats).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 11-25-2017, 07:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-25T06:24:31-08:00","id":1162448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162431#post1162431\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nobody - including the game - is forcing a particular pacing on you. If you want slower going, slow things down... really that simple. The system is designed to be flexible.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course it's that simple. Why are you even stating the obvious?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The CofD do not assign real world benchmarks to the Skills to say how long it should take to get from 4 to 5. This isn't the WoD where you need four dots to be a doctorate in a Knowledge. Five dots is simply human level mastery with no linked association to something to say it should take the kind of effort you've described. </div> </div> </div>In your mind it makes perfect sense to think that the best a human can do in Medicine can be learnt in the time span your character earns a handful of experience points achieving objectives not related to medicine. It's so clear that downtime is not needed and there's no need for the character to study, he can become the best doctor in one year. Sure. I don't know if you can't get this obvious concept or you're just in bad faith, either case it's obvious that talking to you won't lead anywhere. I'm out :3","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-25T07:52:24-08:00","id":1162461,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162336#post1162336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">18-year-old Masters are only a problem if you feel they are. There is no shortage of fiction about incredibly powerful youth. Learning quickly can be indicative of being talented, a genius, prophecy/destiny, external influence or having latent potential.<br /> <br /> You can decide for yourself if you're okay with a story focused on quick-learners, the talented, and geniuses - or want to table things at a more slow and realistic pace.<br /> <br /> This kind of goes along with the idea of your troupe being among the few cabals that regularly makes tons of progress and advances its goals steadily while many of the NPCs stagnate in the background as a point of reference. I don't strive to keep upgrading NPC stats and getting new equipment and spells for them unless it is related to the story. Even still, NPCs won't progress as much as PCs do. PCs naturally have the &quot;spark&quot; and motivation to figure things out. Whereas NPCs are typically replaced with more competent NPCs (more resourceful or dangerous enemies and allies) or upgraded (merits/resources), if possible.<br /> <br /> Also, being talented or a genius can attract all sorts of admirers and haters - at the end of the day, quick-learner or not, you can still get your legs broke.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm frankly quoting this because I think everyone is operating, at least in this &quot;18 year old Master&quot; paradigm, on an assumption about how the setting works that's wrong and needs to be fought by STs. <br /> <br /> There is no, &quot;Age of Awakening.&quot; You can, though it's probably rare, Awaken at three years old, or you can Awaken at seventy. The former is rare, but there's no actual connection between Age and Magical Power.<br /> <br /> Plenty of new initiates to Orders are taught by people half their age, because there isn't and shouldn't be an assumption that everyone Awakens at roughly 20, give or take. If you want an eighteen year old Master, it isn't really hard? And isn't that much more unlikely than a 50-year old Master. <br /> <br /> I know people are using it a shorthand for, &quot;One year into being a Mage, and already a Master&quot; or whatever, but they really, really, really shouldn't. <br /> <br /> *******<br /> <br /> And it's a good thing that anyone can Awaken at any age, and not just because it allows you to have fun scenes where some thirty-something new Mage is like, &quot;Listen to that kid, hah! As if!&quot; only to learn that no, that nineteen year old is a Status 3 Member of the Order, and about to adjust the ass-to-ears ratio on you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12866-the_laurent","name":"The_Laurent","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-25T12:17:28-08:00","id":1162530,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The_Laurent</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162461#post1162461\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I'm frankly quoting this because I think everyone is operating, at least in this &quot;18 year old Master&quot; paradigm, on an assumption about how the setting works that's wrong and needs to be fought by STs.<br /> <br /> There is no, &quot;Age of Awakening.&quot; You can, though it's probably rare, Awaken at three years old, or you can Awaken at seventy. The former is rare, but there's no actual connection between Age and Magical Power.<br /> <br /> Plenty of new initiates to Orders are taught by people half their age, because there isn't and shouldn't be an assumption that everyone Awakens at roughly 20, give or take. If you want an eighteen year old Master, it isn't really hard? And isn't that much more unlikely than a 50-year old Master.<br /> <br /> I know people are using it a shorthand for, &quot;One year into being a Mage, and already a Master&quot; or whatever, but they really, really, really shouldn't.<br /> <br /> *******<br /> <br /> And it's a good thing that anyone can Awaken at any age, and not just because it allows you to have fun scenes where some thirty-something new Mage is like, &quot;Listen to that kid, hah! As if!&quot; only to learn that no, that nineteen year old is a Status 3 Member of the Order, and about to adjust the ass-to-ears ratio on you.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You would think an 18-year-old Master is mundane compared to the plethora of mysteries and absurd phenomenon. Am I really more bothered by a teen arcane Master than an abyssal incursion threatening to erase and dismantle everything I've ever known? Or people who pull cars and weapons out of their pockets? Eh? Are you serious? In a game where spells can be hidden in video games and sex or torture can be used to empower magic?<br /> <br /> In a game where people can shape-shift, modify their appearance, travel through time, modify the past and future, form contracts with invisible entities with unknown powers, literally change their fate and destinies, and so on.<br /> <br /> Suddenly being old and leathery to be a Master is the one thing we need to hold constant?<br /> <br /> It's fine to have it as somewhat of a baseline but I think it's cool to open the floor for discussion on expectations and leave room to shorten the time frame (as was noted a few posts ago - about lowering the 10 year time frame to 7.5 months with the right resources).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-25T18:43:22-08:00","id":1162593,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162530#post1162530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You would think an 18-year-old Master is mundane compared to the plethora of mysteries and absurd phenomenon. Am I really more bothered by a teen arcane Master than an abyssal incursion threatening to erase and dismantle everything I've ever known? Or people who pull cars and weapons out of their pockets? Eh? Are you serious? In a game where spells can be hidden in video games and sex or torture can be used to empower magic?<br /> <br /> In a game where people can shape-shift, modify their appearance, travel through time, modify the past and future, form contracts with invisible entities with unknown powers, literally change their fate and destinies, and so on.<br /> <br /> Suddenly being old and leathery to be a Master is the one thing we need to hold constant?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well because the former examples are assumptions of the setting. &quot;We play mages, who cast magic and sometimes fight creatures of the abyss&quot; the latter is a somewhat disconnect between setting and mechanics.<br /> <br /> As apparently the term bugs you so much i am gonna use &quot;reach mastery in a year&quot; issue. Sure mechanically you can but setting wise it feels odds, there are plenty of examples of old mages (not in, he awakened as an old man but mages who have had long careers) that are a force to be reckoned but then when your character can accomplish more in less time some people, not all apparently, feel there is a disconnect. Suddenly that old master is not a scary but a very suboptimal NPCs. Specially with already published NPCs. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162336#post1162336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">18-year-old Masters are only a problem if you feel they are. There is no shortage of fiction about incredibly powerful youth. Learning quickly can be indicative of being talented, a genius, prophecy/destiny, external influence or having latent potential.</div> </div> </div> </div>This right here has a &quot;but&quot; in that most of that fiction of incredible powerful youths are special characters and not run of the mill even for their own supernatural societies. Which is fine for some people to play/run, apparently the &quot;you are the hieromagus candidate&quot; is a common premise of games here. But it isnt when the PC are suppose to play nobody special when they arent supposed to play Harry Potter but background Gryffindor student number 1345533.<br /> <br /> I prefer the latter, I make that very clear on my games that PCs arent nothing special and they wont be able to change the setting (as in the world, not the city. The latter is open) in any meaningful way. Part of that is that building to the power of a Master takes time an effort. And the needs of my story require time skips and caps on arcanums.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-25T22:39:23-08:00","id":1162638,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162593#post1162593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well because the former examples are assumptions of the setting. &quot;We play mages, who cast magic and sometimes fight creatures of the abyss&quot; the latter is a somewhat disconnect between setting and mechanics.<br /> <br /> As apparently the term bugs you so much i am gonna use &quot;reach mastery in a year&quot; issue. Sure mechanically you can but setting wise it feels odds, there are plenty of examples of old mages (not in, he awakened as an old man but mages who have had long careers) that are a force to be reckoned but then when your character can accomplish more in less time some people, not all apparently, feel there is a disconnect. Suddenly that old master is not a scary but a very suboptimal NPCs. Specially with already published NPCs.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This right here has a &quot;but&quot; in that most of that fiction of incredible powerful youths are special characters and not run of the mill even for their own supernatural societies. Which is fine for some people to play/run, apparently the &quot;you are the hieromagus candidate&quot; is a common premise of games here. But it isnt when the PC are suppose to play nobody special when they arent supposed to play Harry Potter but background Gryffindor student number 1345533.<br /> <br /> I prefer the latter, I make that very clear on my games that PCs arent nothing special and they wont be able to change the setting (as in the world, not the city. The latter is open) in any meaningful way. Part of that is that building to the power of a Master takes time an effort. And the needs of my story require time skips and caps on arcanums.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It's not really something that irks me. I've enjoyed fiction that plays it fast-and-loose and fiction where things are slow and methodical. If I recall correctly, Harry Potter isn't actually an amazing wizard. He just survived a thing he shouldn't have survived. My Harry Potter friends insist there are many more powerful, intelligent, charismatic, and interesting wizards to be found in those stories. One that comes up often is Hermione.<br /> <br /> It's fairly normal in some anime, or just video games (Skyrim), to fly through magic training and become an ace in a montage or couple of hours of gameplay.<br /> <br /> My players aren't usually interested in min-maxing anyway. They're fairly casual and go for whatever looks interesting or relevant to their character. They're barely interested in combat as it is.<br /> <br /> I run games, and prefer to play games, like Exalted. The sky is the limit. I'm not gonna tell you that you can't do this or that. You can go for whatever you want if that is the type of story you wanna tell. Whether or not you have the resources to make it happen reliably are separate issues. Even if you do something horrible to the setting, that doesn't mean the setting can't react. The setting is always bigger than you and always intended to show you the consequences of your actions.<br /> <br /> If you unleash a deadly plague or resolve to try and kill the local Hierarch, that's cool. If you can make it happen, then I guess you're telling the kind of story you want to see being told. Usually you (the ST) would have informed them (the PCs) of the social nature of CofD games and that something is always lurking just beyond what you can see. Presumably, you (the PC) want to &quot;change the world&quot; in spite of all the negative that could come from even trying to do so.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I don't see the point in restricting things like progression rate - mostly because I'm fine with quick-progression and it doesn't break my immersion, my players aren't known to be min-maxers, and I'll follow whatever rate the table thinks is best.<br /> <br /> If your immersion is broken or your players are known to min-max, then I guess you have all the reason to make changes.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-26T02:41:24-08:00","id":1162667,"title":"","content":"To be fair Exalted's expectations are a little different to chronicles. In exalted you basically play a shonen protagonist and are outright <i>expected</i> to hit the power level that most people take centuries to accrue in maybe a decade, tops.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-26T02:56:29-08:00","id":1162671,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1161577-mages-flat-experience-costs?p=1162593#post1162593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well because the former examples are assumptions of the setting. &quot;We play mages, who cast magic and sometimes fight creatures of the abyss&quot; the latter is a somewhat disconnect between setting and mechanics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where does the setting even imply that most masters are &quot;old mages&quot; that have been around for multiple decades? The game says they exist, and are powerful members of their communities (and maybe some NPCs in the books are poorly designed to be such, but that's unfortunately nothing new)... but that's pretty easy to maintain when you remember there's a hell of a lot more power in the game than your Arcana ratings.<br /> <br /> A really basic example of the difference between a PC that's rushed to master an Arcanum and a mage that's been around for a long time to actualize mastery? Rotes.<br /> <br /> A master that's had time to develop their own Rotes (which isn't free) has a massive advantage over a fresh master that's still relying on Rotes they learned from others: the rote quality for instant casting. As well they're more likely to have versions of their Rotes that maximize their Rote Skills - and have had more time to pump those up to max as well - for even more edge.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of places in the game for more experienced mages to grow horizontally in power that makes them truly scary even if a PC ticks of their 5th dot in an Arcana really fast.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...not all apparently, feel there is a disconnect. </div> </div> </div>I don't believe a true disconnect has actually been highlighted.<br /> <br /> The problem with the essential claim is that it treats something that's possible in the system as something that happens with great frequency in the system. The disconnect only occurs when there's a disconnect between the pace of the action of the game, and the pace of the advancement in the game as it is being run for individual groups (side note, this doesn't even apply to the initial issue of the costs being flat, in 1e if you're handing out 6-7 XP at the equivalent of once per in-game day, characters are going to grow hella fast there too).<br /> <br /> There can't be a disconnect between the setting and mechanics directly when the mechanics don't insist on a particular in-game pace of play. The game doesn't say at all, and most certainly implied quite differently, that every game session should cover one day of in-game time, thus creating extremely rapid growth from an in-game perspective.<br /> <br /> The mechanics do allow for characters to grow rapidly, and that's completely on purpose because the designers want people that like faster advancement to be able to run that sort of game too. As well, the disconnect isn't setting vs. mechanics, but the group in question as you yourself highlight;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This right here has a &quot;but&quot; in that most of that fiction of incredible powerful youths are special characters and not run of the mill even for their own supernatural societies. Which is fine for some people to play/run, apparently the &quot;you are the hieromagus candidate&quot; is a common premise of games here. But it isnt when the PC are suppose to play nobody special when they arent supposed to play Harry Potter but background Gryffindor student number 1345533.<br /> <br /> I prefer the latter, I make that very clear on my games that PCs arent nothing special and they wont be able to change the setting (as in the world, not the city. The latter is open) in any meaningful way. Part of that is that building to the power of a Master takes time an effort. And the needs of my story require time skips and caps on arcanums. </div> </div> </div>The game doesn't presume you're playing a super-unique special snowflake plot armor driven character. But the game does assume you're playing the protagonists in your story, not a background character. The player characters are supposed to be &quot;special&quot; in at least their potential compared to average folks. So the disconnect here is not setting vs. mechanics, but the default assumptions of what kind of role the PCs are supposed to have in the game. The books default towards them being exceptional types (even if not to the extremes of the main characters of lots of fiction), where you're talking about them not being exceptional. The advancement the game allows doesn't have an inherent disconnect if you follow the base assumption that the PCs are main protagonists in their story with the uniqueness that implies, so running into a disconnect implies that you're putting the PCs in a position the game didn't expect you to.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}